{'id':'F01','time':'00:00–00:11.32','type':'Confirmed editorial timing mismatch','priority':'high','observation':'The visual MARKUS6/HATAM title ends at00:02.50. The recorded spoken chapter name occurs at00:07.067–00:09.759, after the title is gone and Jesus arrival is already shown. Verse1 begins around00:11.323.','evidence':'Actual final opening/title frames and saved source title567–3259ms/verse1start4823ms, plus6500ms film offset. Earlier chapter lesson af4c7eef-1681-42aa-b5d6-208410bded6d requires chapter names to be treated as editorial material beforeverse1.','proposedCorrection':'Synchronize the title display with the existing spoken chapter name and let arrival coververse1, preserving all audio. Exact opening structure to agree with Dan before editing.'},
{'id':'F02','time':'00:37.30–01:07.65;02:02.69–02:49.34','type':'Performance judgment','priority':'medium','observation':'The spokesperson and Jesus use very broad mouth shapes, raised brows and closed-eye/smiling expressions during extended speech. The commissioning close-ups around02:03–02:07 and02:16–02:17 are conspicuous examples. The delivery can read as acted emphasis rather than ordinary direct speech.','evidence':'Actual final midpoint overview and focused encoded-film speech samples122.5–137.5seconds. These still samples establish facial poses; this pass does not independently certify Hatam phoneme accuracy or diagnose a sync error. Earlier user acceptance of some emphasis is retained, not treated as a demand to make all speech neutral.','proposedCorrection':'Choose which lines should stay emphatic and which need calmer brow/head/mouth treatment. Make selective changes only after comparing Dans comments; no blanket lip-sync regeneration.'},
{'id':'F03','time':'00:13.50–00:34.30;03:11.84–03:55.87','type':'Editing/pacing judgment','priority':'medium','observation':'The teaching view remains almost the same for about21seconds. Herods introduction then spends about44seconds alternating largely passive throne portraits with little new visual information. Separate generated takes still feel repetitive when assembled.','evidence':'Actual final timeline and midpoint boards; the Herod sequence contains distinct selected clips, so this is not a claim of exact copied frame loops. The external review agrees on slow Herod coverage but its raw312/334seconds fields were malformedHHMM and normalized from its written03:12–03:34 evidence.','proposedCorrection':'Vary meaningful coverage while retaining the exact source recording and pauses. Do not simply trim narrated Scripture or invent a new event to fill the time.'},
{'id':'F04','time':'01:21–01:28;05:07–07:32;10:32–10:40;13:15–13:23','type':'Continuity/identity ambiguity','priority':'medium','observation':'The recognizable grey-bearded elder in rose/cream appears as a Nazareth healing recipient, resembles a royal banquet guest, appears among the fed crowd, and is the final Gennesaret healing recipient. The same recognizable supporting faces and village-style wardrobe recur across settings. This can suggest one character moving between unrelated roles or being healed twice.','evidence':'Nazareth/feeding/Gennesaret use the same actual elder canonID d32771a2-2cdd-452b-a825-bacd5f932a6c; actual banquet frames show a matching white-bearded rose/cream guest. The guest is not separately named in the principal casting list, so the banquet identity comparison is visual, not a claimed named biblical role.','proposedCorrection':'Agree whether these are intentionally recurring people or reused extras. If different people, differentiate faces/wardrobe in the revision. This is a readability concern, not a claim Scripture forbids a person appearing twice.'},
{'id':'F05','time':'05:07.41–05:47.45','type':'Art direction judgment','priority':'medium','observation':'Herod is visibly royal, but the small set of plainly dressed, recognizable crowd guests makes the birthday gathering read more like a village meal than a gathering of nobles, commanders and Galilean leaders.','evidence':'Actual banquet establishing/midpoint frames. This is not a factual demand for a specific number of guests, military uniforms, crowns or unsupported luxury.','proposedCorrection':'Consider more distinct formal guest styling and clearer banquet social staging, while preserving approved principal canon and period setting.'},
{'id':'F06','time':'06:54.45–07:32.18','type':'Narrative coverage judgment','priority':'high','observation':'A guard exits, but the later return, platter transfer and burial account are mainly covered by bowed Herod, a daughter portrait, Herodias and the repeated banquet wide. The major narrated events are difficult to follow visually.','evidence':'Actual encoded-film16frame sequence405–450seconds and timeline414.448–452.182. Earlier brief labels describe a return and handoff, but the delivered pictures do not clearly show those actions. Narration is preserved; this does not mean Scripture/audio was omitted.','proposedCorrection':'Consider clear non-graphic coverage of the stated events, such as an appropriately framed or covered platter/handoff and a restrained John-related transition. No graphic execution, invented action, added dialogue or new footage without agreement.'},
{'id':'F07','time':'11:47.23–11:51.23','type':'Confirmed image-quality compromise','priority':'medium','observation':'The walk-on-water cut changes from the wide image to a noticeably enlarged, softer feet detail. The detail is a640x360crop enlarged to1280x720.','evidence':'Actual final water samples and retained component metadata: first native0–95wide, then96–191 cropped x256/y344/640x360. This is continuous source footage, not a replay. Water splashes are visible, so the external reviewers blanket missing-displacement claim is not confirmed.','proposedCorrection':'Prefer a detail with adequate native resolution or a less aggressive crop/replacement angle, if Dan also finds the softness distracting. Do not add arbitrary visual effects or change the miracle action merely to address an unconfirmed contact claim.'}
